---
import LinkCard from '../components/ui/LinkCard.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/ui/PageHero.astro';
import Panel from '../components/ui/Panel.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pageSeo } from '../seo/page-config';

const seo = pageSeo.protectEnvFilesAndSecrets;
---

<BaseLayout {...seo}>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <div class="page-container-compact">
      <PageHero
        centered={false}
        title="How to Protect .env Files, API Keys & Config Secrets on macOS"
        subtitle="A practical developer playbook for sharing environment variables, database credentials, and signing certificates safely using drag-and-drop OpenPGP encryption."
      />

      <Panel tone="strong" class="p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">Why `.env` files are the #1 source of accidental credential leaks</h2>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            Every software project relies on local configuration files that intentionally stay out of Git version control: <code>.env.local</code>, <code>.env.production</code>, Firebase service-account JSON files, Apple `.p12` signing certificates, Android keystore files, and SSH bastion keys. Because these files are listed in <code>.gitignore</code>, developers still need a way to hand them over when onboarding a new teammate, working with a contractor, or backing up a project.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, teams frequently take dangerous shortcuts: pasting plaintext <code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code> or <code>DATABASE_URL</code> values into Slack direct messages, attaching raw <code>.env</code> files to email threads, or dropping them into shared Google Drive folders. Years later, if a single contractor’s email or chat account is compromised, attackers simply search the message history for <code>BEGIN PRIVATE KEY</code> or <code>_SECRET=</code> and harvest live production credentials.
          </p>
          <p>
            Encrypting those files locally on your Mac with <strong>Moaiy</strong> before they ever touch Slack, email, or cloud storage eliminates that persistent exposure window in less than ten seconds.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">Comparing ways developers share `.env` files and secrets</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[620px] text-left text-sm text-text-secondary">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-line-subtle text-text-primary">
                <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-heading">Sharing Method</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-heading">Exposure Risk in Transit / Storage</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-heading">Works Offline & Across Orgs?</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-heading">Cost & Setup Overhead</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="border-b border-line-strong">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Plaintext in Slack / Email</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Critical—secrets remain searchable in cloud logs forever</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Yes, but insecure</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Zero setup, high breach risk</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-line-strong">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Enterprise Secret Managers</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Low, but centralized in a third-party cloud SaaS</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Requires seat licenses for external contractors</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Paid per-user monthly seats</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-line-strong">
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Command-Line `gpg` / `sops`</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Very low (standard OpenPGP encryption)</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Yes, universal standard</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">High CLI friction for non-DevOps teammates</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-text-primary">Moaiy on macOS</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Very low—encrypted locally into standard `.gpg` files</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Yes—100% offline & interoperable across platforms</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3">Free & open source (MIT); visual drag-and-drop</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">Step-by-step workflow: Onboarding a teammate or contractor safely</h2>
        <ol class="mt-5 list-inside list-decimal space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Have your teammate export their public key:</strong> Ask your teammate to install Moaiy (or any OpenPGP tool), generate their key pair, and send you their public key file (for example, <code>alex-pubkey.asc</code>). Because a public key can only lock files—never unlock them—they can safely post it right in your team Slack channel or GitHub repo.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Import and verify the fingerprint:</strong> Drag <code>alex-pubkey.asc</code> into Moaiy’s Key Manager and confirm the 40-character fingerprint with Alex during your daily standup call.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Bundle hidden dotfiles cleanly:</strong> Because macOS Finder hides files starting with a dot (<code>.env</code>) by default, press <kbd class="rounded border border-line-strong px-1.5 py-0.5 font-code text-xs">⌘ Cmd</kbd> + <kbd class="rounded border border-line-strong px-1.5 py-0.5 font-code text-xs">⇧ Shift</kbd> + <kbd class="rounded border border-line-strong px-1.5 py-0.5 font-code text-xs">.</kbd> in Finder to toggle hidden files visible—or compress your <code>.env.local</code> and service-account JSON files into <code>project-secrets.zip</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Drag, select Recipient Mode, and click Encrypt:</strong> Drop <code>project-secrets.zip</code> into Moaiy, select Alex’s public key (and your own key if you want to keep an encrypted copy for yourself), and click <strong>Encrypt</strong>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Send `project-secrets.zip.gpg` over any channel:</strong> Even if your Slack workspace or email archive is breached in the future, the <code>.gpg</code> attachment is cryptographically useless without Alex’s private key and passphrase.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">Storing encrypted `.env.gpg` backups inside your Git repository</h2>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            Many indie developers and small engineering teams use Moaiy to maintain a disaster-recovery copy of their environment files right inside their private Git repository without exposing plaintext secrets:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-compact">
            <li>Keep <code>.env</code> and <code>.env.local</code> in your <code>.gitignore</code> file so plaintext secrets are never committed.</li>
            <li>Drag <code>.env.production</code> into Moaiy and encrypt it using <strong>Recipient Mode</strong> for the core maintainers’ public keys (and your offline hardware backup key), producing <code>.env.production.gpg</code>.</li>
            <li>Commit <code>.env.production.gpg</code> to version control. Whenever you set up a new MacBook, simply clone the repo and drop <code>.env.production.gpg</code> into Moaiy to restore your working environment in seconds.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">Developer security checklist for macOS</h2>
        <ul class="list-compact mt-3">
          <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Never reuse production keys in staging:</strong> Maintain separate <code>.env.staging.gpg</code> and <code>.env.production.gpg</code> archives encrypted only for the engineers who need access to each environment.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Rotate secrets when offboarding:</strong> Encryption protects secrets in transit and at rest, but if a contractor leaves the project after decrypting a key, rotate the underlying API token and distribute a fresh <code>.gpg</code> bundle to active team members.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-text-primary">Pair with a YubiKey for production keys:</strong> Store your personal decryption subkey on a hardware security token so malware cannot quietly copy your private key from your Mac’s SSD.</li>
        </ul>
      </Panel>

      <section class="link-grid">
        <LinkCard href="/guides/" title="All Guides" description="Browse the full macOS encryption knowledge base." />
        <LinkCard href="/smartcard-encryption-guide/" title="YubiKey & Smart Cards" description="Isolate developer private keys on hardware tokens." />
        <LinkCard href="/verify-openpgp-fingerprints/" title="Verify Fingerprints" description="Confirm teammate public keys before sending secrets." />
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
